eady gave the port, so do not call this tool for that server.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__report_preview_port", "schema": {"description": "Tell the person's app which port on this session's machine serves a web page they may want to look at. The person may be following this session from a phone or a browser, where a preview pane can show that page. The app does not know the port unless you say so.\n\n- Call this when a dev server or other web server is up and the person may want to see its page, or when you are about to point them to one that was already running. Give the port it really listens on: read it from the server's output, not from what you asked for, since many servers move to the next free port.\n- Call it once for each server, and again only if that server's port changes. A restart on the same port needs no new call.\n- When more than one server runs, give each a short name, such as \"web\" or \"docs\".\n- Do not call it for a database, a debugger, an API with no page, a port nothing listens on, or a server you start only for your own tests.\n- This tool only states the port: it starts nothing and opens nothing. The app decides what to show and the person decides whether to open it. Not every app shows a preview, so do not tell the person one is ready because you called this.\n- If a tool whose name ends in preview_start started the server, that call already gave the port, so do not call this tool for that server.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"name": {"description": "A short name for the server, such as \"web\", when more than one runs. An app may show it to the person.", "maxLength": 40, "type": "string"}, "port": {"description": "The port the server really listens on, on this session's machine.", "maximum": 65535, "minimum": 1, "type": "integer"}}, "required": ["port"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__report_preview_port"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Connect THIS session to a folder the user's own computer serves through Remote Control, so this session's commands and file tools can run there. Calling this tool IS the request, and a person answers it every time (no permission mode and no stored rule does). So do NOT ask in chat first; once the task needs something that only exists on the user's computer, call list_computer_folders, then call this tool with the chosen folder's environment_id, computer_name and folder copied exactly — the request is refused if they do not match the folder's record. Only a folder under `folders` in that list can be requested; for one under `cannot_be_used`, give the user its explanation instead. The call waits for the computer. This can take a minute. The computer answers only while the Claude desktop app, or Claude Code's Remote Control, is running there. The result is final: `attached`, `refused` (the computer said no, and the result says why), or `did_not_answer` (no computer attached: it did not answer in time, or the user cancelled the request while it waited; nothing is left pending). A session is attached to at most one folder at a time, and it stays tied to the first computer it attaches to: to use a different computer, the user starts a new session. Asking again for the folder the session is attached to reconnects it: that attachment ends first, then the computer is asked afresh. While this session is attached to a folder (list_computer_folders shows it as `current`), do not call this tool for a different folder, and while an earlier request is still waiting, do not call it at all: the user would be shown the permission prompt, and the request is refused even if they allow it. If the user wants a different folder, tell them which computer and folder this session is connected to, and that they can disconnect it (how is below) and then ask you again. If the request is refused or the computer does not answer, do not re-ask in chat; carry on without it and say what remains blocked, including what the refusal says. You have no tool that disconnects the computer: only the user can. If they ask how: from this conversation's menu or its connected-computer indicator on claude.ai; or on that computer, wherever they set up Remote Control there (in the Claude desktop app: Settings, Claude Code, Remote Control; if the folder is served by the `claude remote-control` command, by stopping that command, which disconnects every session using the folder). The desktop app's settings page is also where they choose which folders are in the list.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__request_computer_folder", "schema": {"description": "Connect THIS session to a folder the user's own computer serves through Remote Control, so this session's commands and file tools can run there. Calling this tool IS the request, and a person answers it every time (no permission mode and no stored rule does). So do NOT ask in chat first; once the task needs something that only exists on the user's computer, call list_computer_folders, then call this tool with the chosen folder's environment_id, computer_name and folder copied exactly — the request is refused if they do not match the folder's record. Only a folder under `folders` in that list can be requested; for one under `cannot_be_used`, give the user its explanation instead. The call waits for the computer. This can take a minute. The computer answers only while the Claude desktop app, or Claude Code's Remote Control, is running there. The result is final: `attached`, `refused` (the computer said no, and the result says why), or `did_not_answer` (no computer attached: it did not answer in time, or the user cancelled the request while it waited; nothing is left pending). A session is attached to at most one folder at a time, and it stays tied to the first computer it attaches to: to use a different computer, the user starts a new session. Asking again for the folder the session is attached to reconnects it: that attachment ends first, then